# # # Compositional fields # # #

# The quaternions output by the bingham average are interpolated to the grid
# Quaternions can be written with a scalar part q.w
# and a vector part (q.n) or (q.x, q.y, q.z).
# If we want to map the average rotation of another mineral we can interpolate
# cpo mineral 1 q.w[0] ... to the grid as well.
# The field "eigenvalue a1" is there to use the base interpolation scheme on one other field
# It also tells us about how "peaked" the distribution for the a-axis is
# or in other words how well the a-axes align.
# water is required for the drex algorithm to work.
subsection Compositional fields
  set Number of fields = 6
  set Names of fields = qw, qx, qy, qz, \
                        eigvalue_a1, water
  set Compositional field methods = particles, particles, particles, particles, \
                                    particles, static
  set Mapped particle properties = qw:cpo mineral 0 q.w[0], qx:cpo mineral 0 q.n[0], qy:cpo mineral 0 q.n[1], qz:cpo mineral 0 q.n[2], eigvalue_a1:cpo mineral 0 eigenvalues a axis[0]
  set Types of fields = generic, generic, generic, generic, \
                        generic, generic
end

# For crystal preferred orientation mapping we often start from a random state.
# To have a smooth start, it is favorable to choose the identity quaternion
# q = (1, 0, 0, 0) as the initial condition.
subsection Initial composition model
  set List of model names = function

  subsection Function
    set Function expression = 1; 0; 0; 0;\
                              1/3; 0
  end
end

# # # Discretization # # #

# For quaternions it is recommended to use discontinuous degree 0 polynomials,
# in other words a constant value per cell.
# This ensures that we recover a proper rotation after interpolation.
# We can set different discretization and polynomial degrees
# for the properties that are not quaternions if we want to.
subsection Discretization
  set Composition polynomial degree = 0, 0, 0, 0, \
                                      2, 0
  set Use discontinuous composition discretization = true, true, true, true, \
                                                     false, true,
end
